/*
    UIChat.uss
    Layout for the chat panel. Colours come from FishMMO-Theme.uss.
*/

/*
    Anchored bottom-left. Each panel is its own UIDocument whose root fills the screen, so a
    panel with a size but no position resolves to the top-left corner rather than anywhere
    meaningful — which is where this sat.

    LIFTED clear of the bottom-centre block. It was at bottom:40, which put its bottom-right
    corner under the hotkey strip: measured at 1200x800, .hotkey-list resolves to x294..906 while
    this panel is x24..444, so the two overlapped by 150x32px — the first three slots drawn over
    the chat log. A centred 12-slot strip is 612 wide and cannot clear a 420-wide bottom-left
    panel on a 1200-wide screen at any slot size, so the chat log is what has to move, and it has
    moved twice since: to 104 when the resource bars went side by side into that column, and to
    132 when the buff/debuff strips were put in the band above the bars.

    The 104 was set by the resource row. The row is 612 wide and so reaches into x294..444, this
    panel's column — the old 280-wide stack was chosen specifically to stop short of it. The log
    gave way instead and took the 6px of gutter that was left over.

    The 132 is set by the strips, which now run the full width of the screen at y678..694 on an
    800-unit panel and pass underneath this one. Their top edge is 10px below this panel's bottom
    edge, and they are the only thing there — the cast bar next to them is x500..700 and this log
    stops at x444, so the two never meet.

    A FIXED 222, not `height: 280; max-height: 34%`. The band this panel may occupy is bounded at
    both ends by bottom-anchored panels — the strip band below it and the toast stack at 360
    above it — so it is 360 - 132 - 6 = 222 units at EVERY aspect ratio, and a percentage cap
    makes that band depend on the screen's height instead: at 675 units tall (16:9) a 34% cap bit
    at 229 and the log floated 20 units shorter than it needed to, and at 800 the 280 stood. Both
    numbers were always the same distance from the bottom edge; only one of them knew it.

    None of this moved the panel's TOP edge, which is 354 units up and is what .toast-stack's 360
    offset was measured against; each time the log gave ground it did so downward.

    UIToast.uss anchors its stack off this panel's top edge and UIParty.uss off its own top, so
    both carry offsets derived from these numbers.
*/
.chat-panel {
    position: absolute;
    left: 24px;
    bottom: 132px;
    width: 420px;
    height: 222px;
}

.chat-tabs-bar {
    flex-direction: row;
    height: 24px;
    padding-left: 4px;
    padding-right: 4px;
}

.chat-tabs {
    flex-direction: row;
    flex-grow: 1;
    overflow: hidden;
}

.chat-tab {
    margin-right: 2px;
    font-size: 11px;
    padding-left: 8px;
    padding-right: 8px;
}

.chat-add-tab {
    width: 24px;
    font-size: 14px;
}

.chat-scroll {
    flex-grow: 1;
    padding: 2px 4px;
}

.chat-messages {
    flex-direction: column;
}

.chat-message {
    flex-direction: row;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    margin-bottom: 1px;
}

/*
    middle-left on both halves of a line. A row is as tall as its tallest label,
    and a flex row stretches the shorter one to match -- a Label draws its text at
    the TOP of that stretched box by default, which is what made every line sit
    high in its own space. The two labels also differ in height whenever one wraps
    and the other does not, so without this the sender and the message it belongs
    to do not sit on the same baseline.
*/
.chat-message__name {
    /* Sized from C#, not here — see the note below .chat-message__text. */
    -unity-font-style: bold;
    -unity-text-align: middle-left;
    margin: 0 4px 0 0;
    padding: 0;
}

/*
    THE SIZE IS NOT HERE, on purpose.

    Both halves of a line are sized from C#, by UITKChat reading ClientChatSettings.FontSize —
    the player's "Message Font Size" in the Options UI tab — and writing it inline on each label.
    This rule used to carry a hard-coded 12px, and leaving one here now would be a second source
    of truth for the same number: it would silently disagree with the setting the moment the
    player moved the slider, and the panel would draw a size nothing had chosen. A value read
    per row is also what makes a message arriving after the change come out the right size.

    The rest of the box is this file's business, though, and what follows is why the log reads
    the way it does.

    The box chrome is zeroed, and that is the whole reason a single 12px line was costing 29px
    of log.

    Measured on a rendered panel: the label resolved to padding 1,4,2,4 and margin 2,4,4,2 —
    eleven pixels of vertical box around a twelve-pixel line — and the row came out 29px tall with
    a 1px gap under it. NONE of it is in this file. Unity's default runtime theme applies it to
    every text element, and because the theme's own header promises it carries no layout
    properties, nothing here had ever contradicted it: the two message rules set an alignment and
    a font style and left the rest to a stylesheet the panel does not own.

    Left alone, the density of the log is decided by a default nobody chose. Zeroed, the line box
    is the glyphs plus their natural leading, which is what every other dense list in this game
    already gets -- the party rows and the attribute rows both set their own padding for the same
    reason.
*/
.chat-message__text {
    -unity-text-align: middle-left;
    white-space: normal;
    flex-shrink: 1;
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
}

/*
    No fixed height. The row used to be pinned to 26px with the field given flex-grow, and under
    that constraint the TextField's inner text element resolved to 380x0 — correct colour, correct
    font size, no height, so nothing was drawn and every keystroke went in invisibly. The login
    fields differ only in this: they size to their own content and have always rendered normally.
    Letting the row do the same is the shape that is known to work, rather than another attempt to
    force the inner element to fill a box it will not fill.
*/
.chat-input-row {
    min-height: 26px;
    padding: 2px 4px;
    flex-shrink: 0;
    /* UITK defaults flex-direction to column, not row as CSS does. With a single child that was
       invisible; the moment the channel selector joined the field it stacked above it instead of
       sitting beside it. */
    flex-direction: row;
    align-items: center;
}

.chat-input {
    flex-grow: 1;
}

/*
    Unity's default vertical padding on the glyph box makes the field taller than
    the 12px line it holds. Trimmed so the input row sits closer to the height of
    the text in it -- the same treatment the guild roster search needed, and safe
    for the same reason: the glyphs never used that space.
*/
.chat-input > #unity-text-input {
    padding-top: 0;
    padding-bottom: 0;
}

/*
    Channel selector sitting to the left of the input. It names the channel a plain line will be
    sent on, so it has to hold its width rather than be squeezed by the field beside it — the
    input carries flex-grow, and without flex-shrink: 0 here the label collapses as soon as the
    panel narrows.
*/
.chat-channel-selector {
    flex-shrink: 0;
    min-width: 54px;
    height: 20px;
    margin-right: 4px;
    padding: 0 6px;
    font-size: 10px;
}


/*
    "New messages" pill. Sits over the bottom of the message list rather than in
    the layout, so showing it never reflows the list underneath and never changes
    where the reader is looking. Only ever visible while the reader has scrolled
    away from the bottom and something arrived below them.
*/
.chat-new-messages {
    position: absolute;
    bottom: 6px;
    align-self: center;
    padding: 2px 10px;
    font-size: 10px;
    border-radius: 9px;
}

.chat-new-messages--hidden {
    display: none;
}
